# The processes this corpus actually holds, at every level. One row per level: who acts, what they
# act on, what comes out, and whether examples/diagramming emits it.
#
# The tables not carried are a different process
#   They are not empty of content. `coupling_search`, `stack_scope`, `bound_origin`, `narrowing` and
#   `claim`'s provenance columns have no element in the supply process: diagrams/domain_objects.sqlc
#   renders the first two as documentation only and files the rest as `none`. They have an actor, a
#   subject and an outcome of their own: somebody looked, at something, and found something. That is
#   a process, the one about the act of filing rather than about the supply. `pm.claim` carries
#   `prov_entered_by` and `prov_approved_by`, which is two roles and an order, so the supply has no
#   sequence and this level has one.
#
# The levels above share one shape
#   Every level above the first has the same shape, three times over:
#     checks/all      rule | filing, layer | violates | detail
#     arithmetic/all  site | filing, layer | verdict  | detail
#     algebra/all     law  | subject       | holds    | detail
#   Actor, subject, outcome, why. Nobody designed that; it came from each roster answering the same
#   question one level up. It is the mark of a process about a process, and it is already data.
#
# The levels end on an empty table
#   Each level's output is the next one's subject: a figure becomes a claim, a claim becomes a
#   verdict, a verdict becomes a law's subject, a law becomes a contract's subject. Then
#   reports/integrity.sqlc is required to return nothing, so a level 5 would have no population at
#   all. The levels have a start and an end, for the same reason the parts do.
#
# Only level 0 is emitted
#   That is the finding rather than a limit. Many of these queries reach no drawable table at all,
#   and `epistemics/` is wholly among them: the whole record of who looked at what has no BPMN
#   element. It is not undrawable because it is vague. It is undrawable because it is a different
#   process, and this emitter renders one.
-- the levels of process this corpus holds, and which of them a BPMN emission carries.
SELECT * FROM (VALUES
  (0, 'supply',   'an operation, in the lane it draws from', 'a layer''s supply',
      'a draw, and a commitment induced on another layer',   'pm.operation, pm.draw, pm.induction', true),
  (1, 'filing',   'a party, an enterer, an approver, a searcher',
      'the figures level 0 leaves behind',
      'a claim with a standing, a search with an outcome, a scope with an extent',
      'pm.claim provenance, pm.absence, pm.coupling_search, pm.stack_scope, pm.bound_origin, pm.narrowing', false),
  (2, 'checking', 'a rule on checks/roster.sqlc', 'the claims level 1 filed',
      'violates, or NULL for a rule that examined nothing',  'checks/all.sqlc', false),
  (3, 'auditing', 'a law on algebra/roster.sqlc, a site on arithmetic/roster.sqlc',
      'the relations level 2 rests on', 'holds, or a verdict',
      'algebra/all.sqlc, arithmetic/all.sqlc', false),
  (4, 'contract', 'a roster, against the population it declares', 'the laws level 3 states',
      'nothing. An empty result is the end condition, which is why there is no level 5',
      'reports/integrity.sqlc', false)
) AS l(level, process, actor, subject, outcome, where_it_lives, emitted)
